        "type": "paragraph",
        "snippet": "Both ACE inhibitors and ARBs (Angiotensin II receptor blockers) are recommended as first-line medications to treat hypertension (high blood pressure), but generally offer a better safety and tolerability profile.",
        "snippet_highlighted_words": [
          "generally offer a better safety and tolerability profile"
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          0,
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Comparison of ACE Inhibitors and ARBs"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Efficacy: Both drug classes lower blood pressure effectively and protect the kidneys and heart similarly.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects (ACE inhibitors): These drugs frequently cause a persistent dry, hacking cough and carry a small risk of (severe swelling of the tissues).",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Side Effects (ARBs): These medications block the same pathway downstream without causing the dry cough, making them much easier for patients to tolerate long-term.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              4,
              5
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Usage Restrictions: Neither ACE inhibitors nor ARBs should be taken during pregnancy due to the risk of birth defects, and they should never be combined together for high blood pressure treatment ."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          6,
          7
        ]
